        "original_id": "fab68125-1bbb-5013-a81b-6cd57c270322",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Roger Angell; Harry Stephen Keeler\n- Works: Season Ticket: A Baseball Companion; Five Seasons; Late Innings; The Summer Game\n- Organizations: The New Yorker; The New York Times\n- Topics: American essayists; sports writing; baseball; fiction; non-fiction; criticism; mystery; science fiction\n\nFacts:\n- Harry Stephen Keeler was born on November 3, 1890, and he was a prolific but little-known American author of mysteries and science fiction.\n- Roger Angell was born on September 19, 1920, and he is an American essayist known for writing on sports, especially baseball.\n- Roger Angell wrote numerous works of fiction, non-fiction, and criticism.\n- Roger Angell wrote an annual Christmas poem for The New Yorker for many years.\n- Roger Angell wrote the 1988 book Season Ticket: A Baseball Companion.\n- Before Season Ticket: A Baseball Companion, Roger Angell's previous works included Five Seasons, Late Innings, and The Summer Game.\n- The Summer Game was a New York Times best-seller.\n- Harry Stephen Keeler died on January 22, 1967.",
